WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.438
Welcome to Part 1 of the implementation
series for non-person identity.

2
00:00:04.438 --> 00:00:06.988
When a person leaves, HR tells you.

3
00:00:06.988 --> 00:00:10.125
When a service account's owner
leaves, nothing tells you.

4
00:00:10.125 --> 00:00:14.412
So this track has to build the record
that HR provides for everyone else.

5
00:00:14.412 --> 00:00:16.412
The guide does that in six gates.

6
00:00:16.412 --> 00:00:19.545
For each gate, we'll cover
what done means, the steps,

7
00:00:19.545 --> 00:00:24.663
the commands from the developer workbook's
first labs and what they really print,

8
00:00:24.663 --> 00:00:28.177
the federal requirement and
the evidence an ISSO keeps,

9
00:00:28.177 --> 00:00:32.225
what's available in the government
clouds, and how to roll it back.

10
00:00:32.225 --> 00:00:37.323
Part 1 takes the first three gates:
inventory every non-person identity,

11
00:00:37.323 --> 00:00:41.125
decide each one's target,
and set the credential rules.

12
00:00:41.125 --> 00:00:45.490
Part 2 takes least privilege,
conversion and the lifecycle,

13
00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:49.588
and then SQL Server, AI
agents, evidence and rollback.

14
00:00:49.588 --> 00:00:51.975
Design guidance is labeled as such.

15
00:00:51.975 --> 00:00:54.500
And the workbook's code is illustrative.

16
00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:58.380
It's tested against fixtures,
not against a live tenant.

17
00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:04.217
Here are all six gates on one page.

18
00:01:04.217 --> 00:01:08.108
The first is done when every
non-person identity is listed,

19
00:01:08.108 --> 00:01:11.755
typed, and joined to a record
with an owner and a sponsor.

20
00:01:11.755 --> 00:01:15.597
The second is done when every
identity has a target and a date,

21
00:01:15.597 --> 00:01:17.518
with high-risk accounts first.

22
00:01:17.518 --> 00:01:22.786
The third is done when the tenant blocks
new secrets and caps credential lifetimes,

23
00:01:22.786 --> 00:01:26.756
and existing credentials sit in
a vault on a rotation calendar.

24
00:01:26.756 --> 00:01:28.205
Part 2 picks up from there.

25
00:01:28.205 --> 00:01:31.167
High-privilege grants
are justified or removed.

26
00:01:31.167 --> 00:01:36.454
Converted identities sign in without stored
secrets, after a clean parallel run.

27
00:01:36.454 --> 00:01:39.136
And the paths for owner changes, orphans,

28
00:01:39.136 --> 00:01:42.630
reviews and decommissioning
run and leave records.

29
00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:46.355
Each gate names the read-only
validation kit checks that prove it.

30
00:01:46.355 --> 00:01:47.780
One note on numbering.

31
00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:50.855
The workbook's chapters use
the project plan's gates.

32
00:01:50.855 --> 00:01:55.141
Its Gate 0 and Gate 1 both fall
inside the guide's first gate,

33
00:01:55.141 --> 00:01:57.460
and its Gate 2 is the second.

34
00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:06.185
Before the first gate, agree the record
that every non-person identity joins to.

35
00:02:06.185 --> 00:02:11.148
For this track, the source of truth is
the owning system's record, not HR.

36
00:02:11.148 --> 00:02:15.236
The system inventory is the
master, and that's design guidance.

37
00:02:15.236 --> 00:02:19.660
Each record carries a system or
application ID as the join key.

38
00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:23.125
Entra apps point at it through
a service management reference,

39
00:02:23.125 --> 00:02:26.923
which Microsoft recommends because
it survives one person leaving.

40
00:02:26.923 --> 00:02:31.935
The record also names two technical owners,
and a sponsor who is a federal employee.

41
00:02:31.935 --> 00:02:36.750
It holds the identity's type and object
ID, its target and decision date,

42
00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:40.485
its credential and where that's
vaulted, and a risk tier.

43
00:02:40.485 --> 00:02:43.023
Collecting all this
needs reader roles only.

44
00:02:43.023 --> 00:02:47.497
The checks sign in with a certificate
or a managed identity, never a secret.

45
00:02:47.497 --> 00:02:49.322
Most of this track is free.

46
00:02:49.322 --> 00:02:52.436
Workload ID Premium
adds Conditional Access

47
00:02:52.436 --> 00:02:55.385
and ID Protection for workload identities.

48
00:02:55.385 --> 00:03:00.480
And in GCC, Entra ID runs in
Microsoft's commercial cloud.

49
00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:06.192
The first gate starts with a warning.

50
00:03:06.192 --> 00:03:11.193
Consent creates service principals, and
Microsoft's own services provision more.

51
00:03:11.193 --> 00:03:15.930
So the inventory is always larger than
the list of apps anyone asked for.

52
00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:19.820
In Active Directory, the read finds
user accounts used as services,

53
00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:23.992
and the managed, group managed and
delegated managed service accounts.

54
00:03:23.992 --> 00:03:29.392
It records passwords that never expire,
password age and service principal names.

55
00:03:29.392 --> 00:03:34.129
Treat stale as a lead, not a verdict,
until sign-in logs confirm it.

56
00:03:34.129 --> 00:03:38.230
Record which hosts can retrieve each
group managed account's password.

57
00:03:38.230 --> 00:03:40.792
That list is its real blast radius.

58
00:03:40.792 --> 00:03:44.017
In Entra, record each
service principal's type.

59
00:03:44.017 --> 00:03:47.819
A legacy one can hold credentials
with no app registration,

60
00:03:47.819 --> 00:03:49.992
so an app-only review never sees it.

61
00:03:49.992 --> 00:03:52.979
List managed identities with
their role assignments.

62
00:03:52.979 --> 00:03:57.330
User-assigned ones outlive their resources,
so they're the type that orphans.

63
00:03:57.330 --> 00:04:01.218
Export every federated
credential's issuer and subject.

64
00:04:01.218 --> 00:04:06.710
And list AI agents, including the ones
that run as ordinary service principals.

65
00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:14.735
Next, every identity joins to a
record in the system inventory.

66
00:04:14.735 --> 00:04:19.035
For an app registration, use its service
management reference where it's set.

67
00:04:19.035 --> 00:04:23.123
Otherwise, search by name and
owner, and then interview people.

68
00:04:23.123 --> 00:04:26.786
Find the application owner before
anyone touches the account.

69
00:04:26.786 --> 00:04:30.110
An account nobody will own
becomes a retirement candidate.

70
00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:32.921
Every app registration
and service principal

71
00:04:32.921 --> 00:04:35.660
gets two human owners,
never service accounts.

72
00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:40.373
Keep that list short, because an owner
can add a credential and act as the app.

73
00:04:40.373 --> 00:04:43.397
The business approver goes
in the registry instead.

74
00:04:43.397 --> 00:04:48.759
Every identity also gets a sponsor, who
is a federal employee mastered in HR.

75
00:04:48.759 --> 00:04:53.398
A contractor can build or run a
workload, but can't be its only sponsor.

76
00:04:53.398 --> 00:04:56.871
The Federal Modernization Workbook
sets that rule for agents,

77
00:04:56.871 --> 00:04:59.410
and the program applies
it to everything here.

78
00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:02.670
And where the tenant doesn't
expose the agent features,

79
00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:06.150
govern agents as the service
principals they run as.

80
00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:13.188
The workbook's first chapter
builds the register in code.

81
00:05:13.188 --> 00:05:17.021
It starts from Microsoft Learn's
own commands and export script,

82
00:05:17.021 --> 00:05:19.987
kept as pinned copies, and
adds what they leave out.

83
00:05:19.987 --> 00:05:24.412
Its lab runs on a fictional estate,
with no tenant and no domain.

84
00:05:24.412 --> 00:05:28.037
One run reads both directories
and writes one register.

85
00:05:28.037 --> 00:05:30.439
Here it finds thirty-five identities,

86
00:05:30.439 --> 00:05:33.538
fifteen in Active Directory
and twenty in Entra.

87
00:05:33.538 --> 00:05:34.988
Eight are high risk.

88
00:05:34.988 --> 00:05:38.584
There are eight findings, eleven
warnings, and three accounts

89
00:05:38.584 --> 00:05:42.675
handed to the Data Platform plan,
because they belong to SQL Server.

90
00:05:42.675 --> 00:05:45.400
The second command lists
the high-risk rows.

91
00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:49.112
A sync account's password hasn't
changed in twenty-eight hundred days.

92
00:05:49.112 --> 00:05:51.838
A backup account sits in Backup Operators.

93
00:05:51.838 --> 00:05:55.362
A web front end is trusted
for unconstrained delegation.

94
00:05:55.362 --> 00:05:58.388
And one app holds a
directory-wide write permission.

95
00:05:58.388 --> 00:06:02.233
One account was found only because
its password never expires,

96
00:06:02.233 --> 00:06:05.780
so it may be a person, and
someone confirms that first.

97
00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:12.742
The second chapter checks
ownership against that register.

98
00:06:12.742 --> 00:06:14.556
It reads each owner once,

99
00:06:14.556 --> 00:06:18.818
and counts an owner only if it's
a user, a member, and enabled.

100
00:06:18.818 --> 00:06:21.843
A guest or another service
principal doesn't count.

101
00:06:21.843 --> 00:06:23.656
Against the fictional estate,

102
00:06:23.656 --> 00:06:27.718
it checks twenty-two identities
and finds seven orphan candidates.

103
00:06:27.718 --> 00:06:31.243
Two apps have no valid owner,
and four have only one.

104
00:06:31.243 --> 00:06:33.831
Six have no service management reference,

105
00:06:33.831 --> 00:06:37.604
and three point at a record that's
free text, retired or unknown.

106
00:06:37.604 --> 00:06:41.342
Five Active Directory accounts
have no valid application owner.

107
00:06:41.342 --> 00:06:44.918
Then it writes the orphan rule
as a plan, and sends nothing.

108
00:06:44.918 --> 00:06:47.877
There are ten actions,
and three are writes:

109
00:06:47.877 --> 00:06:51.006
two disables, and one
owner added from a claim.

110
00:06:51.006 --> 00:06:54.280
Another claim is refused,
because it named a guest.

111
00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:56.905
A reviewer approves the plan by its hash.

112
00:06:56.905 --> 00:07:02.460
The writes themselves belong to the orphan
rule in gate six, which Part 2 covers.

113
00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:08.447
Here's the federal side of the first gate.

114
00:07:08.447 --> 00:07:13.075
Account management treats non-person
identities as a defined account type,

115
00:07:13.075 --> 00:07:14.985
each with an owner and a sponsor.

116
00:07:14.985 --> 00:07:20.423
The evidence is this gate's record, plus
the kit runs for owners and agent sponsors.

117
00:07:20.423 --> 00:07:24.743
Identifier management means each
identity has its own identifier,

118
00:07:24.743 --> 00:07:27.135
joined to its record and never reused.

119
00:07:27.135 --> 00:07:29.760
To validate, four read-only checks run.

120
00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:35.061
One lists Active Directory accounts whose
passwords never expire or are too old,

121
00:07:35.061 --> 00:07:37.910
each joined to a record
or listed as unowned.

122
00:07:37.910 --> 00:07:43.235
One reports zero apps with no owner, and
a dated finding for any with just one.

123
00:07:43.235 --> 00:07:47.148
One lists every managed identity
and its role assignments.

124
00:07:47.148 --> 00:07:52.160
And one lists agent identities with their
sponsors, where the tenant exposes them.

125
00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:55.897
The inventory's Graph reads are
listed for the government clouds.

126
00:07:55.897 --> 00:07:59.927
Agent identities are generally
available in commercial,

127
00:07:59.927 --> 00:08:02.110
and in GCC with documented gaps.

128
00:08:02.110 --> 00:08:06.560
And there's nothing to roll back,
because the inventory only reads.

129
00:08:10.310 --> 00:08:14.110
The second gate gives every
identity one of five ends.

130
00:08:14.110 --> 00:08:15.110
Retire it.

131
00:08:15.110 --> 00:08:18.333
Convert it to a group
managed service account,

132
00:08:18.333 --> 00:08:21.810
or a delegated one on a
Windows Server 2025 domain.

133
00:08:21.810 --> 00:08:26.710
Or move it to a managed identity, a
federated credential, or a certificate.

134
00:08:26.710 --> 00:08:30.055
The workbook's migration matrix
decides by account type,

135
00:08:30.055 --> 00:08:31.835
and by where the workload runs.

136
00:08:31.835 --> 00:08:33.385
No stored secrets.

137
00:08:33.385 --> 00:08:36.122
Managed identities for workloads in Azure.

138
00:08:36.122 --> 00:08:40.525
Federation where the workload
already has a trusted issuer,

139
00:08:40.525 --> 00:08:44.486
such as GitHub Actions,
Kubernetes, Google Cloud or AWS.

140
00:08:44.486 --> 00:08:47.173
And certificates only
where neither applies.

141
00:08:47.173 --> 00:08:51.140
A managed service account is upgraded
to a group managed one first,

142
00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:54.386
because neither can move to
a delegated account later.

143
00:08:54.386 --> 00:08:57.940
An app with a client secret
moves to a managed identity,

144
00:08:57.940 --> 00:09:00.310
then federation, then a certificate.

145
00:09:00.310 --> 00:09:02.072
High-risk accounts go first.

146
00:09:02.072 --> 00:09:04.384
And plan ahead for group managed accounts.

147
00:09:04.384 --> 00:09:06.582
They need a key distribution root key,

148
00:09:06.582 --> 00:09:10.780
and domain controllers can wait up
to ten hours after it's created.

149
00:09:14.530 --> 00:09:18.327
The workbook's third chapter
holds the matrix as reviewed data,

150
00:09:18.327 --> 00:09:20.080
and keeps three things apart.

151
00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:23.355
The proposal is the matrix's
target for each account.

152
00:09:23.355 --> 00:09:27.184
The disposition is the
application owner's decision:

153
00:09:27.184 --> 00:09:30.230
retire, re-platform, or
keep, with an end date.

154
00:09:30.230 --> 00:09:32.842
And the gate is the ISSO's.

155
00:09:32.842 --> 00:09:36.430
The lab runs the first two
chapters, then the matrix.

156
00:09:36.430 --> 00:09:40.142
Twenty-one identities are in
scope, and twenty get a proposal.

157
00:09:40.142 --> 00:09:43.718
Eighteen of twenty-two have a
decision, and two waves are planned.

158
00:09:43.718 --> 00:09:46.530
The gate isn't met, and
the findings say why.

159
00:09:46.530 --> 00:09:50.142
A high-risk sync account sits
in wave two, not wave one.

160
00:09:50.142 --> 00:09:51.918
One account has no decision.

161
00:09:51.918 --> 00:09:56.168
An account kept on Kerberos has no
owner for its service principal name.

162
00:09:56.168 --> 00:10:01.018
A kept delegated account has no end
date before Active Directory retires.

163
00:10:01.018 --> 00:10:04.993
One app has no proposal, because
nobody recorded where it runs.

164
00:10:04.993 --> 00:10:07.181
Wave two has no rollback owner.

165
00:10:07.181 --> 00:10:09.732
And a multitenant app
is on the premium list,

166
00:10:09.732 --> 00:10:12.500
though Conditional Access can't cover it.

167
00:10:16.250 --> 00:10:19.987
The guide's evidence table maps
by requirement, not by gate.

168
00:10:19.987 --> 00:10:22.651
So which rows this gate
feeds is our reading.

169
00:10:22.651 --> 00:10:26.898
The decisions set the order that
authenticator management asks for:

170
00:10:26.898 --> 00:10:30.625
managed identities and federation
first, and no new secrets.

171
00:10:30.625 --> 00:10:35.475
Retire decisions lead to disabled accounts,
which account management asks for.

172
00:10:35.475 --> 00:10:38.179
And the fourth phase of
the workbook's directory

173
00:10:38.179 --> 00:10:40.613
migration is its gate
for service accounts.

174
00:10:40.613 --> 00:10:45.163
To validate, every row in the record
has a target and a decide-by date.

175
00:10:45.163 --> 00:10:48.280
The kit check for Active
Directory accounts shows

176
00:10:48.280 --> 00:10:51.175
every high-risk one in the
first wave, or retired.

177
00:10:51.175 --> 00:10:54.287
The check for secrets lists
each app that holds one.

178
00:10:54.287 --> 00:10:58.449
Each has a credential target, or an
exception that says why it can't move.

179
00:10:58.449 --> 00:11:01.729
Workload ID Premium is
available in Azure Government

180
00:11:01.729 --> 00:11:04.862
through the account manager,
but its free trial isn't.

181
00:11:04.862 --> 00:11:08.554
The interim path is an account
kept on Kerberos, islanded,

182
00:11:08.554 --> 00:11:12.638
with an owner and an end date
the authorizing official accepts.

183
00:11:12.638 --> 00:11:14.513
To roll back, re-decide.

184
00:11:14.513 --> 00:11:16.640
The record keeps the history.

185
00:11:20.390 --> 00:11:22.790
The third gate sets the credential rules.

186
00:11:22.790 --> 00:11:24.765
They're design guidance from the books.

187
00:11:24.765 --> 00:11:26.590
No long-lived secrets.

188
00:11:26.590 --> 00:11:31.490
Managed identities first, then federated
credentials, then certificates.

189
00:11:31.490 --> 00:11:35.302
And every certificate in a
vault, on a rotation calendar.

190
00:11:35.302 --> 00:11:39.003
Step one turns on the tenant's
default app management policy.

191
00:11:39.003 --> 00:11:42.056
It blocks new password
secrets and symmetric keys,

192
00:11:42.056 --> 00:11:45.328
and caps new certificates at
a hundred and eighty days.

193
00:11:45.328 --> 00:11:47.815
Those are Microsoft's
recommended settings.

194
00:11:47.815 --> 00:11:51.940
Each restriction applies only to
apps created after a chosen date,

195
00:11:51.940 --> 00:11:54.340
so set it to the policy's effective date.

196
00:11:54.340 --> 00:11:57.890
The panel shows the workbook's
diff against a fictional tenant.

197
00:11:57.890 --> 00:12:01.319
Its default policy is off, so
turning it on is a change,

198
00:12:01.319 --> 00:12:06.902
and every restriction is an add, for both
application and service principal objects.

199
00:12:06.902 --> 00:12:10.652
A restriction the tenant already
had is kept, never dropped.

200
00:12:10.652 --> 00:12:14.614
Existing workloads rotate at
least yearly while they're fixed.

201
00:12:14.614 --> 00:12:19.390
New ones start under the cap, which
addresses the SCuBA certificate policy.

202
00:12:23.140 --> 00:12:26.252
The rest of the third gate is
about where credentials live.

203
00:12:26.252 --> 00:12:31.391
Keep private keys in Azure Key Vault or
a hardware module, never on a file share.

204
00:12:31.391 --> 00:12:35.917
Key Vault renews a certificate
automatically only when it's self-signed,

205
00:12:35.917 --> 00:12:39.690
or comes from a partnered
authority, DigiCert or GlobalSign.

206
00:12:39.690 --> 00:12:43.217
Certificates from an agency's
own authority aren't renewed,

207
00:12:43.217 --> 00:12:45.953
though the vault can
email before they expire.

208
00:12:45.953 --> 00:12:48.102
And a renewed certificate in the vault

209
00:12:48.102 --> 00:12:50.653
doesn't change the app
registration by itself.

210
00:12:50.653 --> 00:12:53.789
Microsoft's own procedure
for SQL Server on Arc

211
00:12:53.789 --> 00:12:56.702
uploads the new certificate
as a separate step.

212
00:12:56.702 --> 00:13:01.040
So automate that upload, or make it
a named owner's task with a date.

213
00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:03.303
A client secret is an exception.

214
00:13:03.303 --> 00:13:06.121
The governance office
and the CISO approve it,

215
00:13:06.121 --> 00:13:09.952
it lasts at most six months, and
it's never renewed automatically.

216
00:13:09.952 --> 00:13:13.034
A traditional account that
has to stay gets a long

217
00:13:13.034 --> 00:13:15.965
random password in the
vault, an owner and a date.

218
00:13:15.965 --> 00:13:19.370
And a new federated credential
is a credential change,

219
00:13:19.370 --> 00:13:22.070
approved and recorded like any other.

220
00:13:25.820 --> 00:13:29.574
The workbook's fourth chapter holds
the policy as a reviewed file,

221
00:13:29.574 --> 00:13:32.632
compares it with the tenant,
and plans the difference.

222
00:13:32.632 --> 00:13:37.182
Against the fictional tenant, the
diff has six adds and one change.

223
00:13:37.182 --> 00:13:40.104
The plan has eight writes:
the default policy,

224
00:13:40.104 --> 00:13:45.395
one dated exception policy and its assignment,
and the property lock on five apps.

225
00:13:45.395 --> 00:13:49.570
Nothing is sent, and the write path
can't loosen or drop a restriction.

226
00:13:49.570 --> 00:13:53.557
Then the rotation plan joins Key
Vault to the app registrations.

227
00:13:53.557 --> 00:13:58.345
A rotation is raised sixty days before
expiry, and an owner approves it.

228
00:13:58.345 --> 00:14:00.145
The vault makes the next version.

229
00:14:00.145 --> 00:14:02.895
The new certificate goes
on beside the old one.

230
00:14:02.895 --> 00:14:05.909
The owner confirms the
workload signs in with it,

231
00:14:05.909 --> 00:14:08.470
and seven days later
the old one comes off.

232
00:14:08.470 --> 00:14:13.920
Here, one certificate renews in the vault,
one is attached, and one is removed.

233
00:14:13.920 --> 00:14:18.670
One app's certificate isn't in the vault
at all, and three apps still hold secrets.

234
00:14:18.670 --> 00:14:21.490
Secrets are replaced, not rotated.

235
00:14:25.240 --> 00:14:29.390
On the federal side, authenticator
management is the main requirement.

236
00:14:29.390 --> 00:14:31.619
The evidence is the policy export,

237
00:14:31.619 --> 00:14:36.078
the kit runs for credentials and
secrets, and the rotation calendar.

238
00:14:36.078 --> 00:14:39.337
The policy also addresses
three SCuBA policies:

239
00:14:39.337 --> 00:14:43.739
no app passwords, and capped
password and certificate lifetimes.

240
00:14:43.739 --> 00:14:47.820
They're SHOULD policies in ScubaGear,
not required configurations,

241
00:14:47.820 --> 00:14:50.152
and their report is filed with the gate.

242
00:14:50.152 --> 00:14:52.822
Audit requirements cover
credential changes,

243
00:14:52.822 --> 00:14:56.314
so the entries for added
credentials are logged and reviewed.

244
00:14:56.314 --> 00:14:59.462
To validate, the credential
check shows nothing issued

245
00:14:59.462 --> 00:15:02.540
after the effective date that
runs longer than the cap.

246
00:15:02.540 --> 00:15:07.602
Every credential that expires within sixty
days has an owner and a renewal date.

247
00:15:07.602 --> 00:15:12.221
The secrets check shows each app still
on a secret is in a conversion wave,

248
00:15:12.221 --> 00:15:14.453
or has an exception with an end date.

249
00:15:14.453 --> 00:15:16.430
App management policies are free,

250
00:15:16.430 --> 00:15:19.891
and Microsoft lists their calls
for the government clouds.

251
00:15:19.891 --> 00:15:22.731
To roll back, give one
application a policy

252
00:15:22.731 --> 00:15:26.670
exception with an end date,
in the exception register.

253
00:15:30.420 --> 00:15:35.320
To close Part 1, here's what each gate
leaves behind, and where Part 2 picks it up.

254
00:15:35.320 --> 00:15:38.256
The first gate leaves
the record, with owners,

255
00:15:38.256 --> 00:15:42.620
sponsors and risk, plus the register
and the SQL Server hand-off.

256
00:15:42.620 --> 00:15:46.432
Gate four restores a removed
grant only from that export.

257
00:15:46.432 --> 00:15:48.657
Gate six runs the orphan rule.

258
00:15:48.657 --> 00:15:51.943
The second gate leaves a target
and a date for every row,

259
00:15:51.943 --> 00:15:55.520
the waves and their rollback,
and the premium license scope.

260
00:15:55.520 --> 00:15:58.334
Gate four puts the most
important single-tenant

261
00:15:58.334 --> 00:16:01.007
service principals
under workload policies.

262
00:16:01.007 --> 00:16:05.470
Gate five converts wave by wave,
with a thirty-day parallel run.

263
00:16:05.470 --> 00:16:08.996
The third gate leaves the
policy, the rotation calendar,

264
00:16:08.996 --> 00:16:11.295
the exceptions and the SCuBA result.

265
00:16:11.295 --> 00:16:16.044
Gate five drops a secret only after the
new credential carries real traffic,

266
00:16:16.044 --> 00:16:18.758
and gate six checks
credentials every week.

267
00:16:18.758 --> 00:16:22.112
To build Part 1's code,
use the developer workbook,

268
00:16:22.112 --> 00:16:25.633
Building Non-Person Identity,
chapters one to four.

269
00:16:25.633 --> 00:16:28.310
Each has its labs and a narrated tour.
